HW-023  ES8311: "the software reset deafens the ADC below ~22 kHz" is WRONG, and the
        driver's whole design premise rested on it.

        M5Stack StickS3 (3rd unit, COM35), Zephyr 4.4.0, 2026-07-12.

        There is no low-sample-rate cutoff. There never was. The ADC takes about six
        seconds of WALL CLOCK to wake up after the codec's analog state is disturbed, and
        the rate sweep had been reading its own elapsed time as a frequency response.

        The conclusion "do not reset this part" survives. Every reason given for it does
        not. And the architecture it was used to justify -- "therefore configure() programs
        every register it depends on" -- was never actually implemented: thirteen registers
        are never written at all.

WHAT WAS CLAIMED
----------------
evidence/20260712-hw019-reset-then-a-deaf-adc-SUPERSEDED.log concluded:

    "the software reset deafens the ADC below ~22 kHz; root cause unknown"

and drivers/audio/es8311.c, dts/bindings/audio/everest,es8311.yaml and upstream PR
#113312 were all written around it. The evidence was this, and it is a clean-looking
curve:

    8000: tone_rms=0    16000: 10    24000: 424    32000: 3721    48000: 15372

A reviewer objected that the claim was over-broad: what had actually been measured was
`reset + a configure() that never writes 0x0B 0x0C 0x0F 0x10 0x11 0x18 0x19 0x1A 0x33
0x34 0x35`, so the reset might have broken nothing and merely taken away a state the
driver was silently depending on. He was right that the claim was unsupported. His
mechanism was wrong, and so was mine -- but chasing it is what found the real one.

1. THE REVIEWER'S HYPOTHESIS -- REFUTED
---------------------------------------
Same sweep, plus the 0x00 reset AND a write of every register configure() never writes,
with 0x10 = 0x1F and 0x11 = 0x7F set to the values ESP-ADF (the firmware these boards
ship with) uses. 0x10 and 0x11 were read back on silicon to prove the writes landed, and
0xFA was checked to prove the register file was not being held down.

    Fs      no reset              reset + EVERY missing register
    8000    floor=93  rms=777     floor=0    rms=0
    16000   floor=101 rms=1381    floor=0    rms=9
    24000   floor=83  rms=2830    floor=4    rms=341
    32000   floor=81  rms=3383    floor=21   rms=2765
    48000   floor=116 rms=14459   floor=116  rms=14990

Indistinguishable from reset-only. Writing every missing register does not rescue the
ADC. That closes the hypothesis. It should also have closed the question -- and it did
not, because of what those numbers look like once you stop reading them as a frequency
axis.

2. THE CONFOUND -- THE SWEEP HAS ONLY EVER RUN IN ONE ORDER
-----------------------------------------------------------
8000, 11025, 12000, 16000, 22050, 24000, 32000, 44100, 48000. Every run, since the sweep
was written. That is increasing sample rate. It is also, unavoidably, increasing time
since the reset, at about two seconds per rate.

So "deaf below 22 kHz" and "deaf for the first ten seconds" fit the same nine numbers,
and no run ever made could tell them apart. Reversing the table separates them, and there
is no third outcome:

    reset, then sweep BACKWARDS -- 48 kHz measured FIRST, 8 kHz LAST

    48000  floor=0    tone_rms=1        <- always alive before. now DEAF.
    44100  floor=0    tone_rms=3
    32000  floor=0    tone_rms=6
    24000  floor=0    tone_rms=19
    22050  floor=1    tone_rms=80
    16000  floor=5    tone_rms=278
    12000  floor=33   tone_rms=1027
    11025  floor=118  tone_rms=2007
    8000   floor=103  tone_rms=923      <- always deaf before. now ALIVE, and back at
                                           the no-reset baseline (93 / 777).

The deafness follows the clock. It does not follow the rate.

This also rules out the codec's own power-up stage timers (0x0B, 0x0C), which are counted
in LRCK periods: if the wake were a fixed LRCK count, 48 kHz -- the fastest frame clock,
measured first -- would have reached it soonest. It was the deafest. The effect is
wall-clock, so it is an analog time constant, not a digital counter.

3. HOW LONG, AND IS IT THE ANALOG BIAS?
---------------------------------------
Reset, configure once, START once, one fixed rate. Nothing reconfigured, nothing
restarted, so the only thing changing is the codec.

    t (ms)   A: reset only    B: reset + 0x10=0x1f, 0x11=0x7f
    543         0                0
    3103        0                0
    3615        1                1
    4639        6                5
    5663       35               37
    6175      101               85     <- awake (the settled floor here is 81..116)
    7199      110              118
    9759      113              108

    A: ADC reached a real noise floor after 6175 ms
    B: ADC reached a real noise floor after 6175 ms

The curves lie on top of each other. 0x10 = 0x1F -- maximum IBIAS_SW, the value ESP-ADF
writes and this driver never does -- changes nothing. About 6.2 seconds, and no register
found so far shortens it.

4. SO CAN THE DRIVER GET A DETERMINISTIC REGISTER STATE FOR FREE?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
"The reset is harmless, so just reset in init()" does NOT follow, and would be a
regression. A warm reboot does not reach the codec's power, so a warm-booted board's
microphone works immediately today. Put a reset in init() and every warm reboot buys six
seconds of deaf microphone it did not previously cost. That is an enormous number for an
audio codec.

But a reset is only ever a SHORTCUT to a known state. Writing the registers is the
explicit route -- and it is free, IF writing them does not itself disturb the analog.
That is the only question that matters, and it is the one this measures.

Each arm proves the chip is awake BEFORE it touches it, then disturbs it exactly one way,
then looks immediately. "awake after N ms" is therefore also how long the PREVIOUS arm's
damage took to clear -- which is the number that catches an arm whose own reading lied.

    arm                            awake after   floor, measured immediately
    D0 configure only (control)       512 ms     103      FREE
    D1 + digital registers            512 ms     123      FREE
    D2 + analog registers             512 ms     109      FREE
    D3 0xFA pulse + everything        513 ms     1024     (see below)
    D4 0x00 reset + everything       4096 ms     0        COSTS THE SIX SECONDS

D2 is the answer. Writing EVERY register the driver depends on -- the digital ones
(0x18 0x19 0x1A ALC, 0x33 DAC offset, 0x34 0x35 DRC, 0x44 0x45 routing/CSM, and 0xFA)
AND the analog ones (0x0B 0x0C power-up timers, 0x0F low-power modes, 0x10 VMID/IBIAS_SW,
0x11 VSEL) -- with no reset of any kind, reads back a real noise floor immediately. And
D3's precondition then woke in 513 ms, which proves D2 left the part healthy.

D4 is the known-bad control and it behaves: floor 0, and it is the only arm that did.

D3's own reading is NOT trustworthy and is recorded here as a caution. Its floor of 1024
was a transient -- the 0xFA pulse pops -- and the proof is that D4's precondition then
took 4096 ms to see a floor at all, so the chip was actually asleep. A peak-RMS liveness
test can be fooled by a click. It was the NEXT arm's precondition guard that caught it,
which is exactly why that guard exists: two earlier versions of this probe had no guard,
silently measured a codec that a previous arm -- or a previous BOOT -- had left asleep,
and reported it as a result.

Pulsing 0xFA bit 0 therefore also costs a settle. That does not matter, because the
driver does not need it: WRITING 0xFA = 0x00 is in the D1 and D2 sets and is free.

PARTIALLY SUPERSEDED BY HW-026 -- READ THIS FIRST
------------------------------------------------
Everything MEASURED below stands. The EXPLANATION offered for the six seconds -- that the
reset discharges three 1 uF analog reference capacitors and recharging them is the cost --
is REFUTED by evidence/20260713-hw026-es8311-cold-boot-is-not-deaf.log. A genuine cold
power-on charges those same capacitors from zero and the ADC is ALIVE at the earliest
moment it can be sampled (2.2 s), while at that same 2.2 s after a reset the floor is 0.
The reset does something a cold start does not, and what that is remains unknown. The
conclusion -- do not reset this part -- is unchanged and is stronger for it.

CONCLUSION
----------
The conclusion "do not reset the ES8311" stands. Every reason previously given for it
does not.

  - There is no sample-rate dependence. The claim of a cutoff below 22 kHz was an
    artefact of a sweep that only ever ran in one order.
  - The reset is not harmful. It costs an analog settle of about 6.2 seconds, on capture
    only, and that is the whole of it.
  - The settle is not the codec's bias registers and not its power-up timers. It is
    wall-clock and it is consistent with a board-level RC on the microphone bias: cycling
    the codec's L3B analog supply, with no reset at all, reproduces it exactly.
  - The driver does not need a reset. Writing every register it depends on gives a fully
    deterministic state at zero cost, on a live part, measured.

And the sting: the driver's stated architecture -- "configure() must not rely on reset
defaults, and it does not: every route programs both directions in full" -- is false as
written. Thirteen registers are never written at all, including 0x44 bit 7 (ADC2DAC_SEL,
a PLAYBACK control written only on the capture path) and 0xFA, whose bit 0 HOLDS the
entire register file at its defaults for as long as it is set and which the driver can
therefore never recover from. The fix is not to add a reset. It is to actually do what
the driver has been claiming it does.

WHY NONE OF THIS WAS EVER NOTICED
---------------------------------
A warm reset -- west flash, the monitor's reset-on-connect, sys_reboot(), the watchdog --
resets the SoC and does not reach the codec's power. The analog has therefore been
settled for minutes by the time any of these tests run. The six seconds are only paid on
a true cold power-on, and every hardware pass this project has recorded (HW-006, HW-016d,
HW-016e, HW-019, HW-022) either ran warm or was driven by a human pressing a button well
after boot.

WHAT IS STILL NOT KNOWN
-----------------------
- The mechanism of the six seconds. "Probably the microphone bias network's RC" is a
  hypothesis consistent with every measurement here, not a finding: it has not been
  checked against the schematic and no attempt has been made to shorten it.
- Whether a cold-booted board's microphone really is silent for the first few seconds.
  Every measurement here is consistent with it, and cycling the L3B rail reproduces it,
  but that is a proxy. Confirming it needs a physical unplug and replug, which has not
  been done.
- Whether the six seconds affect PLAYBACK as well as capture. The acoustic loopback
  cannot separate a deaf microphone from a dead speaker, and no test here does.

